YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online publication 2025-08-04 and print publication 2025-08-21.},
  keywords = {kant, tp-science-math, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {This Element analyzes Kant's metaphysics and epistemology of the exact science of nature. It explains his theory of true motion and ontology of matter. In addition, it reconstructs the patterns of evidential reasoning behind Kant's foundational doctrines.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{thomson2025opus,
  author = {Thomson, Terrence},
  title = {Metaphysics of Nature and Failure in Kant's Opus postumum},
  publisher = {Bloomsbury Publishing Plc},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.5040/9781350418516},
  note = {SUMMARY: Argues that the Opus postumum is Kant's last attempt to deliver the promised system of speculative reason in a work called metaphysics of nature, and reads the surviving drafts for how they fail to establish that work rather than for projected success. It maintains that this failure gives reason to study the Opus postumum seriously, not to abandon it (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Opus postumum and the late metaphysics of nature: whether the late drafts continue, rupture with or complete the critical system.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 0 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 0 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Bloomsbury. Review found by CrossRef search: Zhang, Graduate Faculty Philosophy Journal (2026). NDPR review: not found.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives 2025 for print and issued dates, without month.},
  keywords = {kant, opus-postumum, tp-science-math, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {Focusing on moments of continuity and rupture, as well as the thin lines of fracture in Critical philosophy itself,Metaphysics of Nature and Failure in Kant’sOpus postumum navigates the rough terrain of Kant’s final thoughts.Terrence Thomson argues thatOpus postumumconstitutes Kant’s last attempt to deliver on the promise of a system of speculative reason in a work called ‘metaphysics of nature’. He journeys through the drafts left on Kant’s desk when he died to encounter their failure to establish this work rather than their projected success. Far from this signaling that we give up on serious study of theOpus postumum, however, Thomson shows how failure is not something Kant shied away from but rather embraced as fuel for his continued thinking. Indeed, it is this failure that opens the door to a greater understanding of the dissonances at the core of Critical philosophy itself. Moreover, it indicates what it means to be in the process of philosophizing and that Kant was well-aware that to think often means to repeatedly fail.
  abstract_source = {s2},
 Suggesting how the very call for a metaphysics to be established upon the Critical philosophy can only ever fail to abide by the threshold it sets up, this book offers a new perspective on the central bind Kant repeatedly confronts inOpus postumumwithin the modern European tradition of philosophy. To deliver the system of metaphysics Kant calls for means to transgress the bounds of Critical philosophy, but to attempt to deliver it is nonetheless an unavoidable part of Critical philosophy itself.},
}

@book{cureton2025sovereign,
  author = {Cureton, Adam},
  title = {Sovereign Reason: Autonomy and Our Interests of Reason},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1093/9780191960253.001.0001},
  note = {SUMMARY: Develops, from a wide range of Kant's texts, a Sovereignty Conception of Reason on which an autonomous person governs herself completely by reason: reason legislates, executes and adjudicates for all our mental powers and for all kinds of rational standards, not just moral ones, and includes substantive interests (in knowledge, enlightenment, happiness, solidarity) and a principle of justifiability. It contrasts this with other theories and applies it to rational principles of beneficence, coercion, deception, friendship, respect, happiness and self-development (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy: Kantian autonomy and practical reason; the book extends traditional Kantian conceptions of rational self-governance and contrasts itself with other prominent theories of reason.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 1 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 2 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Oxford University Press. No review found by CrossRef search or NDPR search.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online publication 2025-01-27 and print publication 2025-04-17.},
  keywords = {kant, pp-ethics, debate-constructivism, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 We often invoke reason, rationality, reasonableness, reasons, and related concepts in common sense. Despite what many serious thinkers claim, the reason of everyday life is far more substantive than cold logic or calculating self-interest. This book explores the idea that our power of reason includes an expansive set of governing abilities, motives, and principles. It develops this novel theory drawing on a wide variety of Kant’s texts and highlighting themes in ordinary ways of speaking and thinking that have not been sufficiently investigated. The book’s unifying idea is of an autonomous person who governs herself completely by reason. The Sovereignty Conception of Reason dramatically extends traditional Kantian conceptions of rational self-governance. We can use reason to govern our many mental powers, not just to make choices. We can legislate, execute, and adjudicate in ourselves all kinds of rational standards, not just moral ones. The Sovereignty Conception of Reason holds that our reason includes substantive interests in various things. Part of having a rational nature is to care about knowledge, enlightenment, happiness, solidarity, and other things for their own sake. The Sovereignty Conception of Reason also holds that our reason includes a principle of justifiability according to which mental acts of all kinds are required by reason if and because they are justifiable to rational people on the basis of their substantive interests of reason. The book contrasts the Sovereignty Conception of Reason with other prominent theories and explores rational principles concerning beneficence, coercion, deception, friendship, respect, happiness, and self-development.},
}

@book{kain2025goodwill,
  author = {Kain, Patrick},
  title = {Kant and the Origins of the Good Will},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1093/9780198960850.001.0001},
  note = {SUMMARY: Investigates the origins and emergence of Kant's moral philosophy and the place in it of the good will and the dignity of humanity, working from early published works, notes and drafts, and student notes of Kant's lectures before the Groundwork (1785). It begins with a reconstruction of Kant's conception of the divine will and then interprets the emergence of his moral philosophy before the Groundwork (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy and the development of Kant's ethics: the pre-Groundwork lecture notes and early writings, the good will and the dignity of humanity.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 0 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 0 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Oxford University Press. No review found by CrossRef search or NDPR search.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online (issued) publication 2025-11-19 and print publication 2025-12-23.},
  keywords = {kant, pp-ethics, precritical, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {Abstract The good will and the dignity of humanity are central, but often overlooked or controverted, elements of Kant’s moral philosophy. This book investigates the origins and emergence of Kant’s moral philosophy and the significance to it of the good will and the dignity of humanity, beginning with some of Kant’s early published works, notes and drafts, and student notes from lectures across various disciplines that he gave over the course of several decades prior to the publication of the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785). The book begins with a historical and philosophical reconstruction of Kant’s conception of the divine will, which reflected, influenced, and informed his developing moral philosophy. The book then provides a detailed interpretation of the emergence of Kant’s moral philosophy prior to the publication of the Groundwork. This study of the place of the good will and the dignity of humanity in Kant’s thought sheds new light on the origins and content of Kant’s published works on moral philosophy in the 1780s.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{timmermann2025lie,
  author = {Timmermann, Jens},
  title = {Kant and the Supposed Right to Lie},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1017/9781108992435},
  note = {SUMMARY: A monograph on Kant's 1797 essay On a Supposed Right to Lie from Love of Humanity, which examines its background (the provocation by Benjamin Constant and his translator Carl Friedrich Cramer), the history of the murderer-at-the-door example (Augustine, Fichte, Michaelis), Constant's version of the case, and Kant's core argument that lying, or a right to lie, would undermine contractual rights and spell disaster for all humanity. It includes a facing-page translation of the essay (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy: the Metaphysics of Morals and the duty of truthfulness; the lying-to-the-murderer debate.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 book-level record not located (title search returned only the Critical Inquiry review); CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 3 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Cambridge University Press. Review found by CrossRef search: Krajewski, Critical Inquiry 52(1) (2025). NDPR review: not found.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online publication 2025-07-18 and print publication 2025-08-31.},
  keywords = {kant, pp-mm-virtue, debate-other-lying, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {In his 1797 essay 'On a Supposed Right to Lie from Love of Humanity', Kant argues that when only a confident lie might save a friend, one must, if asked, reply truthfully and thus betray his hiding-place to the person who wants to kill him. This is the first monograph to explore Kant's essay in detail. Jens Timmermann examines the background of the piece (Kant was provoked by Benjamin Constant and his translator, Carl Friedrich Cramer); the history of the example (which was also discussed by, amongst others, Augustine, Fichte and Johann David Michaelis); the peculiarities of Constant's version of the case; and Kant's core argument against Constant: lying, or a right to lie, would undermine contractual rights and spell disaster for all humanity. This rich, interpretative resource, which includes a facing-page translation of Kant's essay, will be of wide interest to Kant scholars and moral philosophers.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{sabourin2025marriage,
  author = {Sabourin, Charlotte},
  title = {Kant on Marriage},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1017/9781009304559},
  note = {SUMMARY: A Cambridge Element that reconstructs Kant's account of marriage from its context and background conversations and from the conception of equality at its core. That equality does not precede marriage and was inaccessible to much of the German population; the author sets Kant beside the eighteenth-century feminist writers Theodor von Hippel and Marianne Ehrmann and concludes that his conception is compatible with certain egalitarian goals and might improve women's lives in a liberal state (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Practical philosophy and Kant on gender: marriage in the Doctrine of Right and the question of equality between spouses.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 10 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 10 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Cambridge University Press (Cambridge Elements, Elements in the Philosophy of Immanuel Kant, as named in the Kant-Studien review title). Review found by CrossRef search: Brecher, Kant-Studien (2026). NDPR review: not found.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online publication 2025-03-10 and print publication 2025-04-30.},
  keywords = {kant, pp-mm-virtue, pp-right-politics, debate-gender, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {This Element proposes a new understanding of Kant's account of marriage by examining the context and background conversations that shaped its development and by discussing the conception of equality at its core. Marriage as Kant understands it relies on a certain form of equality between spouses. Yet this conception of equality does not precede marriage, and carries important limitations – one of which being its inaccessibility to a significant proportion of the German population at the time. The protections and rights conferred by marriage were thus not accessible to all. Their shared preoccupation with this issue allows the author to put Kant's thoughts in relation with those of eighteenth-century feminist writers Theodor von Hippel and Marianne Ehrmann. Despite these limitations, the author finds that Kant's conception of marriage is compatible with the achievement of certain egalitarian goals, suggesting that it may be able to improve women's lives in a liberal state.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{williams2025incorporated,
  author = {Williams, Garrath},
  title = {Kant Incorporated},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1017/9781009641364},
  note = {SUMMARY: A Cambridge Element arguing that Kant's practical philosophy offers a systematic basis for understanding corporations, such as universities, churches, feudal orders and early overseas business corporations. Corporations bridge the distinctions between ethics and right and between public and private right, and can extend freedom, structure moral activity and aid progress, but the unequal liability of those who exercise corporate powers threatens Kant's principle of innate equality; the Element explores the safeguards needed (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Political and legal philosophy: Kant on corporate bodies, innate equality and public and private right.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 5 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 6 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Cambridge University Press (open access on Cambridge Core per the abstract). No review found by CrossRef search or NDPR search.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online publication 2025-09-30 and print publication 2025-11-30.},
  keywords = {kant, pp-right-politics, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {Corporations are legal bodies with duties and powers distinct from those of individual people. Kant discusses them in many places. He endorses universities and churches; he criticises feudal orders and some charitable foundations; he condemns early business corporations' overseas activities. This Element argues that Kant's practical philosophy offers a systematic basis for understanding these bodies. Corporations bridge the central distinctions of his practical philosophy: ethics versus right, public versus private right. Corporations can extend freedom, structure moral activity, and aid progress towards more rightful conditions. Kant's thought also highlights a fundamental threat. In every corporation, some people exercise the corporation's legal powers, without the liability they would face as private individuals. This threatens Kant's principle of innate equality: no citizen should have greater legal rights than any other. This Element explores the justifications and safeguards needed to deal with this threat. This title is also available as Open Access on Cambridge Core.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{teufel2025teleology,
  author = {Teufel, Thomas},
  title = {Kant and Teleology},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.1017/9781108529617},
  note = {SUMMARY: A Cambridge Element arguing that Kant's mature teleological philosophy in the Critique of the Power of Judgment, and a fortiori his aesthetics and philosophy of biology, cannot be understood without a systematic account of the challenges it answers (a threat of transcendental chaos between sensibility and understanding, and of critical chaos between determination and reflection) and of its innovations (a transcendental conception of purposiveness, a principle of nature's purposiveness, a self-governing power of judgment, and a view on which nature does make leaps); it presents such an account (publisher abstract).

SUBFIELD AND DEBATE: Aesthetics and teleology: purposiveness and reflective judgment in the third Critique and its role in the unity of the critical system.

SIGNIFICANCE INDICATORS: S2 citations 8 and CrossRef is-referenced-by-count 9 (retrieved 2026-10-01). Publisher: Cambridge University Press (Cambridge Elements). No review found by CrossRef search or NDPR search.

YEAR NOTE: CrossRef gives online publication 2025-04-17 and print publication 2025-04-30.},
  keywords = {kant, aes-teleo, type-monograph, Medium, summary-source-abstract},
  abstract = {Kant's mature teleological philosophy in the Critique of the Power of Judgment is predicated on innovations that address a set of unprecedented challenges arising from within critical philosophy. The challenges are (1) a threat of “transcendental chaos” between sensibility and understanding, emerging from the structure of critical epistemology; (2) a threat of “critical chaos” between determination and reflection, generated by Kant's response to that first threat. The innovations include (a) a transcendental conception of purposiveness, (b) a principle of nature's purposiveness based on that conception, (c) a power of judgment governed by that principle, (d) and so governed in an unusual (self-given and self-governing) way, (e) a view on which nature does make leaps. This Element argues that Kant's mature teleological philosophy – and a fortiori Kant's aesthetics and philosophy of biology – cannot be understood without a fully systematic account of these challenges and innovations, and it presents such an account.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

